\begin{definition}[Updatable distributed broadcast encryption]
An updatable distributed broadcast encryption scheme for up to $2^\lambda$ users is a tuple of algorithms $\Pi=(\mathsf{Setup},\mathsf{KeyGen},\mathsf{IncPK},\mathsf{IncSK},\mathsf{Enc},\mathsf{Dec},\mathsf{UpdPK},\mathsf{UpdSK})$. $\mathsf{Setup}(1^\lambda)$ outputs public parameters $\mathsf{pp}$, and $\mathsf{KeyGen}(\mathsf{pp},i)$ outputs a key pair $(\mathsf{pk}_i,\mathsf{sk}_i)$ for user $i$. Writing $S$ for a finite set of active users (a map from indices to public keys), $\mathsf{IncPK}$ and $\mathsf{IncSK}$ update an aggregated public key $\mathsf{apk}$ and a user's aggregated secret key $\mathsf{ask}_i$ whenever a user joins or leaves $S$; $\mathsf{Enc}(\mathsf{pp},\mathsf{apk},\mu)$ and $\mathsf{Dec}(\mathsf{pp},\mathsf{ask}_i,\mathsf{ct})$ encrypt to, and decrypt for, the current group; and the pair $\mathsf{UpdPK}(\mathsf{pp},\mathsf{apk},S)\to(\mathsf{ctref},\mathsf{apk}',S')$, $\mathsf{UpdSK}(\mathsf{pp},\mathsf{ask}_i,\mathsf{ctref},S,\mathsf{pk}_i,\mathsf{sk}_i)\to(\mathsf{ask}_i',\mathsf{pk}_i',\mathsf{sk}_i',S')$ jointly re-randomize every active user's keys given only the current membership $S$, so that after a refresh a corrupted user's new secret key does not help decrypt ciphertexts encrypted to $\mathsf{apk}$ before that refresh. The scheme is correct if every honestly generated ciphertext decrypts under every current recipient's aggregated secret key, and is (statically or adaptively) secure if no efficient adversary can distinguish an encryption of a chosen bit from an encryption of a uniform bit given the view produced by any admissible sequence of key-generation, corruption, add/remove, and refresh queries, provided no user in the group at the time of the challenge was ever corrupted while still in the group. $\Pi$ is \emph{succinct} if $|\mathsf{apk}|$, each $|\mathsf{ask}_i|$, each refreshed key, and $|\mathsf{ctref}|$ are all bounded by a fixed polynomial in $\lambda$, independent of $|S|$.
\end{definition}
